fcfd51b73c5e9654f412ff3c0f630371bd5b30" commit="7300c391978377e9adaf87a96bb2c690421a3b68" commit_message="[autotest] Handle json file parsing error  perf_dashboard_shadow_config.json is deployed by puppet and there is no cq to validate that change. Handle the ValueError in case the json file is malformated.  BUG=chromium:581382 TEST=None  Change-Id: I91fcfd51b73c5e9654f412ff3c0f630371bd5b30 Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/324101 Commit-Ready: Dan Shi &lt;dshi@google.com&gt; Tested-by: Dan Shi &lt;dshi@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Kevin Cheng &lt;kevcheng@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="324101" owner_email="dshi@google.com" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/third_party/autotest" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/autotest" ref="refs/changes/01/324101/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-3.18" change_id="Ibf2003be17aed991568d98dfe50bbe72ef5a8292" commit="f046a6e7507ea5f59e96101183424d458eedf8f4" commit_message="CHROMIUM: bq27x00: Fix OOB write in model name  The BQ27500_MAX_NAME_LEN is now 7 and when we are unable to read the model name we strcpy &quot;unknown&quot; to the model name variable which will cause out of bound write because of \0 at the end of the string.  To address this issue, this patch does 3 things. - Increase model name max length from 7 to 20 - Truncate model name when length &gt;= 20 - Change strcpy to strlcpy  BUG=chromium:581343 TEST=build / ryu boot  Change-Id: Ibf2003be17aed991568d98dfe50bbe72ef5a8292 Signed-off-by: Puthikorn Voravootivat &lt;puthik@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/324058 Reviewed-by: David Riley &lt;davidriley@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="324058" owner_email="puthik@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="6" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/changes/58/324058/6" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I80431b777147b23ad7db026508015a4d829bf215" commit="9ad457f0c011aa8386b42fe8daf996d48b460ab3" commit_message="Add more timestamps to make boot time analysis easier  This patch adds several more timestamps to distinguish storage device initialization, kernel body load time, kernel verification time and kernel decompression time. It also renames the existing TS_CROSSYSTEM_DATA timestamp to TS_VB_VBOOT_DONE, which is a much better representation of what it actually signifies.  Some of these timestamps rely on details of how vboot calls its callbacks to make sense, and the kernel load timestamp is a particularly nasty hack. Still, I think this is a net benefit since it allows developers to easily tell the big boot time components apart and avoids having to pull timestamp knowledge into vboot. (Also, I'm tired of having to rehack something like this manually on every board I bring up.)  With this patch, all times in the 10+ millisecond range on a normal Chromebook should be properly accounted for (except for TPM operations which are hidden particularly deep within vboot).  BRANCH=none BUG=none TEST=Booted Oak, confirmed that timestamps show up as expected.  Change-Id: I80431b777147b23ad7db026508015a4d829bf215 Signed-off-by: Julius Werner &lt;jwerner@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/323783 Reviewed-by: Aaron Durbin &lt;adurbin@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="323783" owner_email="jwerner@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="3" project="chromiumos/platform/depthcharge" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/depthcharge" ref="refs/changes/83/323783/3" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I06a7ad8a53e553a8e432a6abb5b38c25a98df6c6" commit="e3cb1639cbd15fbdb76b104f5aa95571d98985f9" commit_message="GPIO: Prefer gpio_set_flags  Convert some uses of gpio_set_flags_by_mask to plain gpio_set_flags. The result is usually more readable due to being able to use the GPIO_* enum names, and it removes more instances of port/mask implementation details leaking outside the gpio.c chip specific code.  Signed-off-by: Anton Staaf &lt;robotboy@chromium.org&gt;  BRANCH=None BUG=None TEST=make buildall -j  Change-Id: I06a7ad8a53e553a8e432a6abb5b38c25a98df6c6 Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/323815 Commit-Ready: Anton Staaf &lt;robotboy@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Anton Staaf &lt;robotboy@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Shawn N &lt;shawnn@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="323815" owner_email="robotboy@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/platform/ec" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/ec" ref="refs/changes/15/323815/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><lkgm version="7867.0.0-rc2"/></manifest>